meta {
  name: Get ping
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/health/ping
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Accept: text/plain
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body: eq pong
  res.headers['content-type']: contains text/plain
  res.headers['x-minerva-request-id']: isDefined
}

docs {
  The cheapest proof that the process is alive: `PingUseCase` returns the literal string
  `pong` and touches nothing at all. Cheap enough to run once a second, which is what a
  container or load balancer probe does — `GET /health` is the one to read when you want
  a verdict with a shape.

  The response is `text/plain`, not JSON, so this request overrides the collection's
  `Accept: application/json`. The handler pins its own `Content-Type` with `@Header`, so
  the override changes nothing on the wire — it is here so the request says what it
  expects, and the header assertion catches the day the pinning is dropped and Nest
  starts answering `"pong"` as a JSON string.
}
